
### 1. Modify object.h

Add the ownership flag and make the character pointer const.

```c
struct ObjString {
  Obj obj;
  bool ownsChars;
  int length;
  const char* chars;
};
```

When ownsChars is true, the string owns its character array.
When false, it only references characters stored elsewhere.

### 2. Modify object.c

Replace the string creation helpers with:

```c
ObjString* makeString(bool ownsChars, const char* chars,
                      int length) {
  ObjString* string = ALLOCATE_OBJ(ObjString, OBJ_STRING);
  string->ownsChars = ownsChars;
  string->length = length;
  string->chars = chars;
  return string;
}
```

This function creates an ObjString and stores whether
it owns the character data.

### 3. Modify compiler.c

For string literals, create a string that references
the original source characters without copying them.

```c
static void string() {
  emitConstant(OBJ_VAL(makeString(
      false,
      parser.previous.start + 1,
      parser.previous.length - 2)));
}
```

Since the characters come from the source code, the
string does not own them.

### 4. Modify vm.c

Concatenation still requires allocating a new character
array because it creates a new string.

```c
static void concatenate() {
  ObjString* b = AS_STRING(pop());
  ObjString* a = AS_STRING(pop());

  int length = a->length + b->length;
  char* chars = ALLOCATE(char, length + 1);

  memcpy(chars, a->chars, a->length);
  memcpy(chars + a->length, b->chars, b->length);
  chars[length] = '\0';

  ObjString* result = makeString(true, chars, length);
  push(OBJ_VAL(result));
}
```

The new string owns its dynamically allocated characters.

### 5. Modify printObject() in object.c

Since borrowed string literals may not be null-terminated,
printing must use the stored length.

```c
void printObject(Value value) {
  switch (OBJ_TYPE(value)) {
    case OBJ_STRING:
      printf("%.*s",
             AS_STRING(value)->length,
             AS_CSTRING(value));
      break;
  }
}
```

### 6. Modify memory.c

Only free the character array if the ObjString owns it.

```c
static void freeObject(Obj* object) {
  switch (object->type) {
    case OBJ_STRING: {
      ObjString* string = (ObjString*)object;

      if (string->ownsChars) {
        FREE_ARRAY(char, (char*)string->chars,
                   string->length + 1);
      }

      FREE(ObjString, object);
      break;
    }
  }
}
```
